\begin_layout Abstract
The growing complexity of modern genomic data analysis requires interoperability between different computational environments.
 While Bioconductor has established itself as a the framework for genomic data analysis in R,
 the Python ecosystem has gained significant traction in data science,
 machine learning,
 and artificial intelligence.
 Here,
 we present BiocPy,
 a comprehensive suite of Python packages that ports core Bioconductor data structures that maintain compatibility with their R counterparts.
 BiocPy facilitates seamless cross-language workflows,
 enabling researchers to leverage the strengths of both ecosystems.
 It implements foundational data structures such as BiocFrame,
 GenomicRanges,
 SummarizedExperiment,
 and SingleCellExperiment,
 alongside utilities for direct reading of R data objects via the rds2py package.
 BiocPy enables researchers to combine the statistical rigor of Bioconductor with Python's machine learning capabilities,
 offering a powerful framework for genomic analyses.
 All packages in the BiocPy ecosystem are published to Python's Package Index (PyPI) 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "http://github.com/BiocPy"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bioinformatics workflows increasingly require the integration of diverse computational tools and methods.
 Bioconductor
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "huber2015orchestrating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 with its extensive collection of R packages for genomic data analysis,
 has been the widely used by researchers in the field for nearly two decades.
 It provides a cohesive framework with standardized data representations and analysis tools that allow researchers to seamlessly manipulate and analyze genomic data across multiple workflows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Meanwhile,
 Python has emerged as the dominant language for data science,
 machine learning,
 and artificial intelligence applications.
 Its extensive ecosystem of standard libraries like NumPy,
 pandas,
 scikit-learn,
 and TensorFlow along with a strong community around imaging,
 single-cell
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealt
key "Virshup2021.12.16.473007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and machine learning methods has made it indispensable for modern computational biology.
 However,
 Python lacks standardized genomic data structures equivalent to those in Bioconductor,
 leading to fragmentation and interoperability challenges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This presents a significant challenge:
 researchers often need to choose between Bioconductor's robust statistical methods and genomic tools in R,
 or Python's superior machine learning capabilities and broader scientific computing ecosystem.
 While bridging solutions such as rpy2
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "rpy2"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 exist,
 they require proficiency in both languages and introduce complexity in deployment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To address this gap,
 we developed BiocPy,
 a collection of packages that bring Bioconductor's core data structures to Python.
 BiocPy enables seamless interoperability between R and Python,
 allowing researchers to leverage the strengths of both ecosystems within a unified analytical framework.
 The project includes implementations of fundamental Bioconductor data structures such as 
\emph on
BiocFrame
\emph default
 for R-like dataframes,
 
\emph on
GenomicRanges
\emph default
 for range-based analyses,
 containers for managing experimental data,
 e.g.
 
\emph on
SummarizedExperiment
\emph default
,
 and 
\emph on
SingleCellExperiment
\emph default
,
 along with utilities for direct reading of R data objects through the 
\emph on
rds2py
\emph default
 package.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike other Python bioinformatics libraries that create their own data models,
 BiocPy prioritizes interoperability with Bioconductor by adhering to its design principles.
 This approach enables seamless transition between languages and unlocks new possibilities for integrated and cross language genomic analyses.
 Creating a bridge between computational platforms enables researches to focus their time on biological questions rather than technical challenges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Core Data Structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy implements essential foundational data structures that serve as building blocks for complex genomic data representations.
 These structures closely mirror their Bioconductor counterparts while integrating naturally with the Python ecosystem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
BiocFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocFrame provides a Bioconductor-like data frame class in Python that follows the principle of "what you put is what you get." Unlike pandas DataFrame,
 which modifies column types during input,
 BiocFrame preserves the original data types.
 This is particularly important when representing complex genomic data structures and ensuring interoperability with R/Bioconductor.
 Key differences from pandas include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Preservation of column data types
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Support for nested data structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Compatibility with R/Bioconductor data models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Functional programming paradigm for accessing and setting properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
BiocFrame also provides seamless conversion to and from pandas DataFrame objects,
 allowing researchers to leverage pandas' rich functionality for data manipulation while maintaining compatibility with Bioconductor workflows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The GenomicRanges package implements interval-based genomic coordinate representations,
 essential for many genomic analyses.
 It includes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
IRanges for generic interval arithmetic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
GenomicRanges for representing genomic locations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
SeqInfo for storing chromosome information
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Methods for interval operations (overlap,
 nearest,
 etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This implementation enables Python users to perform the same range-based genomic analyses that have been a cornerstone of Bioconductor workflows in R.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Container Classes for Experimental Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy includes container classes for representing experimental data:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
SummarizedExperiment:
 Represents genomic experiments with features (rows) and samples (columns),
 integrating assay data with annotation metadata
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
RangedSummarizedExperiment:
 Extends SummarizedExperiment with genomic coordinate information for each feature
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
SingleCellExperiment:
 Represents single-cell experimental data,
 with additional slots for reduced dimensions,
 alternative experiments,
 and cell-cell/feature-feature relationships
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
MultiAssayExperiment:
 Manages multi-omic experiments across multiple samples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These container classes follow Bioconductor's design principles where rows represent features and columns represent samples or cells,
 ensuring conceptual consistency across languages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Interoperability Features
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Direct Reading of RDS Files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rds2py package serves as a Python interface to the rds2cpp library,
 enabling direct reading of RDS files in Python without intermediate formats.
 This eliminates data conversion steps and streamlines the transition between R and Python environments.
 Key features include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Support for Bioconductor S4 classes (SummarizedExperiment,
 SingleCellExperiment,
 etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Memory-efficient implementation through memory views,
 avoiding data duplication
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Direct conversion to BiocPy objects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Conversion Utilities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy provides bidirectional conversion utilities for compatibility with other Python ecosystem tools:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Conversion between BiocFrame and pandas DataFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Import/export capabilities for AnnData objects (used by Scanpy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Support for common file formats (CSV,
 HDF5,
 etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Installation and Deployment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All BiocPy packages are published to PyPI and can be installed individually or through the biocpy wrapper package.
 Conda/Mamba configuration files are also provided for creating environments with all BiocPy dependencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Package Design Principles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy follows several key design principles:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Faithful Representation:
 BiocPy classes implement the same interfaces and behaviors as their Bioconductor counterparts,
 ensuring conceptual consistency across languages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Functional Programming Paradigm:
 Like Bioconductor,
 BiocPy uses functional programming patterns for accessing and setting properties,
 avoiding in-place modifications that can lead to unexpected behavior in complex data structures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Python-Native Implementation:
 While maintaining compatibility with Bioconductor,
 BiocPy is implemented as a native Python library,
 following Python best practices and integrating with the broader Python ecosystem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Minimal Dependencies:
 BiocPy maintains a lean dependency footprint,
 using standard scientific Python libraries (NumPy,
 SciPy,
 pandas) while avoiding unnecessary dependencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Comprehensive Documentation:
 Each package includes detailed documentation,
 tutorials,
 and examples to facilitate adoption by the Python bioinformatics community.
\end_layout

